{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 5, :comments ["<b>Down to the firstborn of the maidservant.</b> They raise a difficulty here: why, in the narrative of the actual plague (Exodus 12:29), is it said 'down to the firstborn of the captive,' who is presumably of lower status than the firstborn of the maidservant? And it seems at first glance that this is no difficulty at all, for it could be that at the time this prophecy was spoken there were not yet captives in Egypt — for this prophecy was spoken before the first of the month of Nisan, and during that interval some were taken captive, and then they too were stricken of necessity: either because their fear exacted vengeance for their insult, or because they rejoiced at their calamity before they were taken captive." "Another matter: therefore Moses did not mention the firstborn of the captive before Pharaoh, so that Pharaoh not object: what sin did the firstborn of the captive commit? For the answer 'because their fear exacted vengeance for their insult' would not satisfy Pharaoh, and all the more so the answer that 'they rejoiced at their calamity.' And Pharaoh would be led astray to say: it was not HaShem who brought all this about by way of punishment, but Moses is employing sorcery for a general plague upon all the firstborn of Egypt." "And there are those who say that it is all one and the same, for by day the captives were made to grind at the millstones and at night they were put into the pit, and the prophecy of Moses was spoken by day; therefore he called them 'the firstborn of the maidservant who is behind the millstones' (Exodus 11:5), but the plague took place at night when they were in the pit; therefore they are called 'the firstborn of the captive' (Exodus 12:29)."], :book :exodus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 11, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:19:25.875611675Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}